{% include "kb/_abstraction_rules.j2" %}
Task: produce ONE merged markdown playbook document, and nothing else (no
preamble, no commentary, no code fences around the whole thing). The document
MUST match the exact structure below. A deterministic Python validator will
reject your output if any of these constraints are violated:

- Every `##` section listed below must be present, in order, with the exact
  heading text.
- The `<!-- meta -->` block must contain the keys `slug`, `class_name`,
  `seen`, `created_from`, `last_updated`, each on its own line as `key: value`.
- Symptoms, Triage Procedure, Verification Procedure, and Required Evidence
  must each be non-empty.
- Required Evidence must contain at least one `- [ ]` checkbox item, and
  every item there must be a `- [ ]` checkbox.
- The following vague phrases are FORBIDDEN anywhere in the document:
  "appropriate values", "as needed", "relevant configuration", "if applicable",
  "as appropriate". Use parameterized placeholders (`<ENV_VAR>`,
  `<NAMESPACE>`, `<DEPLOYMENT>`, ...) for incident-specific tokens — those
  are precise, not vague — and write concrete commands and shapes around
  them.

Authoring rules specific to a merge:
- The N input playbooks describe the SAME fault class. Produce one
  consolidated document that subsumes all of them.
- A merge is also a chance to GENERALIZE. If two input playbooks each
  hard-code a different incident-specific value (e.g. one says
  `CHECKOUT_ADDR`, the other says `PAYMENT_ADDR`), that is direct evidence
  the procedure body must be parameterized with `<ENV_VAR>` rather than
  prescribing either literal value. Apply the abstraction rules above when
  reconciling such cases.
- Resolve contradictions in favor of the more concrete SHAPE and the more
  recently observed signal — prefer parameterized commands and described
  evidence shapes over woolly wording, and prefer the latest run's
  observations (stage_outputs and, if present, recovery_summary) when two
  playbooks disagree on shape. "More concrete" means "more precise about
  what to look for", not "names more incident-specific tokens".
- Do NOT lose distinct Known Distractors or Failure Patterns. Carry every
  distinct entry from every input playbook into the merged document,
  rewriting in the parameterized form where the original was incident-
  specific. Deduplicate ONLY true duplicates (same underlying pattern AND
  same lesson). When deduplicating, keep the more general wording so the
  entry generalizes across incidents.
- If any input playbook had a `## References` section or any other
  incident-specific reference, drop it from the merged document; the
  current schema does not include one.
- If the winner playbook is `(none)`, treat the loser playbooks as the only
  source material and synthesize from them.
- The meta block MUST be filled exactly as:
    slug: {{ winner_slug }}
    class_name: {{ winner_class_name }}
    seen: {{ combined_seen }}
    created_from: recovery
    last_updated: {{ current_iso }}
  (Consolidations are conservative: assume worst-case provenance and tag
  `created_from: recovery` even if some inputs were `success`.)

